# frozen_string_literal: true

# Benchmarks, which Duel runs on two versions of the code, the baseline
# (where your branch left main) and the candidate (your changes), and
# fails if the candidate is slower at any of them. Each version's workers
# load <%= required ? "#{required} and " : "" %>every duel/**/*_duel.rb file. See
# https://github.com/sferik/duel-ruby/blob/main/docs/benchmarks.md.
#
# To write benchmarks that tell a regression from noise:
#
# - Benchmark the work that users depend on being fast: the main entry
#   points, and whatever runs in a loop or once per request. Cover typical
#   inputs, and the small and large extremes, as separate benchmarks.
# - Name each one "area: what it does". `duel --filter "^parse:"` picks
#   out an area.
# - Time only the work. Build inputs and read fixtures in `once`, which
#   isn't timed. To prepare each sample's work, take |n| (the
#   iterations) and wrap the preparation in `untimed`.
# - Make every iteration alike: fixed inputs, seeded randomness
#   (Random.new(42)), and caches warmed in `once`. Undo what an
#   iteration changes with an `around_iteration` hook, or put it back
#   with a `before_sample` hook.
# - Aim for microseconds to a few milliseconds an iteration, and split
#   anything slower.
# - Use `only: :count` for work that's mostly waiting (on a socket, say),
#   and `only: :timing` for work on threads of its own.
# - Use only the API that the main branch has: a benchmark that the
#   baseline can't run is skipped.

# An example, to replace with benchmarks of your own code.
duel "example: join words" do
  once { @words = %w[the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog] } # not timed
  @words.join(" ") # timed
end
